const body="<p><strong>Personal Motivation</strong> For a long time I kept returning\nto the same question. It appeared while reading novels, watching films,\nstudying philosophy, and following scientific discoveries. The works\nchanged. The disciplines changed. The question did not. Why do some\nartifacts genuinely reorganize the way reality becomes intelligible,\nwhile others create the appearance of having done so? We have many words\nfor extraordinary achievement: profound, original, complex,\nrevolutionary, brilliant, genius. But these words often collapse very\ndifferent things. A work can be enormously complicated without revealing\nanything new. Another can be almost embarrassingly simple once\nunderstood and nevertheless change the structure through which a problem\ncan be seen. One thinker can display extraordinary intelligence while\noperating entirely inside an inherited framework. Another can notice\nthat the framework itself is the problem. These are not merely\ndifferences of taste. Something different has happened. Eventually I\nfound a simpler question: <strong>What has actually been\naccomplished?</strong> That question became the beginning of this book.\nIt also led somewhere larger than criticism. To know what an artifact\nhas accomplished, we need to ask what representation itself is doing.\nHow does consciousness make reality intelligible? What happens when\nconsciousness becomes aware of the structures through which it sees?\nWhat happens when those structures multiply, become self-conscious, and\neventually learn to imitate the signals by which we once recognized\nunderstanding? The problem is especially visible now, but it did not\nbegin with artificial intelligence. AI has simply made it difficult to\nignore. <strong>Part I — The Map</strong> <strong>Chapter One — False\nDepth</strong> A painting of fire can be beautiful, accurate, even\nmesmerizing. It can reproduce the color of flame, the shape of smoke,\nthe glow against a wall. Yet no one mistakes the painting for\ncombustion. We can place a hand near it and discover that something is\nmissing. Meaning is harder. A sentence can look profound. A theory can\nlook explanatory. A film can look complex. A person can sound\nintelligent. There is no equivalent test of heat. We recognize invisible\nqualities through traces: understanding leaves traces, insight leaves\ntraces, intelligence leaves traces. For most of human history, a\nsophisticated representation also carried a fairly safe assumption:\nsomewhere behind it, a mind had done something sophisticated. But traces\ncan be reproduced. The language of insight can appear without insight.\nComplexity can be genuine while discovery is absent. Ambiguity can be\ncarefully engineered without disclosing anything that was previously\nhidden. A work can become exquisitely self-aware while doing little more\nthan announcing its self-awareness. Once a culture learns what\nprofundity looks like, profundity can acquire a style. The style can\ntravel independently of the operation that originally produced it.\nSometimes the smoke remains after the fire is gone. This does not mean\nthat complexity, ambiguity, symbolism, recursion, or difficult language\nare fraudulent. Quite the opposite. Some of the greatest works require\nthem. The mistake is to treat the presence of the signal as proof of the\noperation. Imagine a novel containing hundreds of correspondences. Names\nmirror names. Images recur. Historical events echo fictional ones.\nNarrators contradict one another. The architecture may be extraordinary,\nand producing it may require enormous intelligence. But connection is\nnot yet discovery. The work may have constructed a brilliant system\ninside terms that remain fundamentally unchanged. Now imagine a second\nartifact, much shorter and less visibly complicated. It reveals that\nseveral phenomena previously treated as separate are consequences of one\nunderlying relation. Once the relation is seen, the old organization of\nthe problem becomes inadequate. The final statement may be simple. The\ncognitive operation is not. Calling both artifacts “intelligent” is not\nwrong. It is simply too coarse for the distinction we need. The problem\nbecomes sharper in an age saturated with representations. Every event\nproduces commentary. Commentary produces interpretation. Interpretation\nbecomes material for further interpretation. The result is not\nnecessarily greater understanding. Sometimes it is only greater\nrepresentational density. Modern culture is unusually good at producing\nthe visible signs of intellectual seriousness: fragmentation,\nabstraction, irony, self-reference, conceptual vocabulary, interpretive\nopenness. A sophisticated audience can be especially vulnerable to\nsophisticated signals because it has learned where to look. The more\ntrained the interpreter, the more elaborate the reading that can\nsometimes be projected onto an object that only weakly constrains it.\nThe distinction I want to preserve is therefore not between difficult\nand easy, or between simple and complex. It is between <strong>what\na</strong> <strong>representation resembles</strong> and <strong>what it\nactually does</strong>. That distinction will eventually lead to a\nframework for evaluating demonstrated cognition in artifacts. But before\nwe can ask what a representation does, we need to understand why\nconsciousness requires representation in the first place.\n<strong>Chapter Two — The Map and the Mirror</strong> Consciousness does\nnot contain the world. Reality contains too much: too much information,\ntoo many relationships, too many possible distinctions. To exist within\nreality at all, consciousness must reduce it without entirely losing\ncontact with it. It needs maps. A map is not a failed territory. It\nsucceeds because it leaves almost everything out. A city map that\ncontained every person, every movement, every changing surface and every\nmoment would eventually need to become another city. The usefulness of\nthe map depends on selection. It preserves certain relations and\ndiscards others. Language does the same. The word <em>tree</em> contains\nneither roots nor leaves nor sunlight nor centuries of biological\nhistory. It removes almost everything. Precisely because it does, we can\ncompare trees, remember absent trees, imagine impossible trees, and\nreason about classes of things that are not present. Representation is\ntherefore not simply a wall between consciousness and reality. It is a\nselective structure through which something becomes cognitively\navailable as something. The reduction costs us something, but it also\nmakes thought possible. The same process occurs at many levels. We do\nnot ordinarily experience air vibrations; we hear a voice. We do not\nattend to individual printed marks; we read a sentence. A skilled reader\nno longer sees letters and then laboriously reconstructs meaning. The\nmarks become transparent. The reader sees a door opening in a dark\nhallway, a face turning away, an argument taking shape.\n<strong>Successful representation tends to disappear into what it\nmakes</strong> <strong>visible.</strong> That disappearance is\nnecessary. A consciousness forced to analyze every mediation through\nwhich experience becomes intelligible would be paralyzed. Human beings\ndo not merely inspect representations. They inhabit them. But\ntransparency creates a danger. The better a representation works, the\neasier it becomes to forget that it is a representation. An\ninterpretation begins to look like reality itself. A category becomes\nnatural. A worldview simply becomes the world. Then something happens. A\ncontradiction appears. Another culture organizes the same phenomenon\ndifferently. A scientific anomaly refuses to fit. A philosopher asks why\nthe obvious category should be obvious. A work of art exposes the\nstructure through which its own reality has been presented. The map\nreappears. Consciousness can now do something peculiar: it can represent\nits own representation. It can think not only about the world, but about\nthe categories, assumptions, symbols, and interpretive structures\nthrough which the world became intelligible. This is recursion. I become\nangry. Then I notice that I am angry. Then I ask whether the anger is\njustified. Perhaps I discover that what felt like righteousness was\nhumiliation. The first-order experience remains, but my relation to it\nhas changed. I am no longer only angry. I am also observing the\nconsciousness that is angry. A similar movement can occur culturally. A\nrule is followed, then its legitimacy becomes a question. A ritual is\ninhabited, then the relation between the ritual and what it claims to\nrepresent becomes visible. A concept such as justice functions fluently,\nthen someone asks what justice actually is. The mirror has turned toward\nitself. This movement creates freedom because a representation that can\nbe seen as representation can be compared, criticized, modified,\nrejected, or replaced. But the same movement creates distance. The\nperson who sees the map can no longer inhabit it in exactly the same way\nas the person for whom the map remained transparent. Reflection\ntherefore produces two possibilities at once: liberation and alienation.\nAnd it creates a problem that will follow the rest of this book:\n<strong>How can consciousness inhabit a map after seeing the\nmap?</strong> <strong>Part II — The History of the Mirror</strong>\n<strong>Chapter Three — From Belonging to Recursion</strong> What\nfollows is not a literal evolutionary history in which one kind of human\nmind replaces another. Ancient people were not incapable of doubt,\nself-awareness, abstraction, or criticism. Mythic, symbolic, recursive,\nscientific, and religious forms coexist in every period, including ours.\nThe history that matters here is a genealogy of representational\nrelations: changes in what can become culturally explicit about the\nstructures through which reality is understood. In a deeply inherited\nsymbolic order, representation may be difficult to experience as one\npossible map among others. Myth does more than offer stories about\nreality. It can place birth, death, authority, weather, ancestry, taboo,\nsexuality, and cosmic order inside a shared field of meaning. The point\nis not whether the cosmology is factually correct. The point is the\nrepresentational operation: a heterogeneous world becomes inhabitable\nthrough a common symbolic structure. Meaning is not continuously\nmanufactured by the individual. Much of it is already there. That does\nnot make such worlds peaceful or just. Integrated worlds can be brutal.\nBut suffering inside an intelligible order differs from suffering in a\nworld whose intelligibility has itself become uncertain. At some point,\nin different places and in radically different forms, the inherited\norder increasingly becomes an object of explicit reflection. There is no\nsingle historical morning when “the mirror” appears. But certain figures\nmake the change unusually visible. Socrates is almost theatrical in this\nrespect. He begins with concepts everyone already uses—justice, courage,\nvirtue, knowledge—and interrupts their fluency. What is justice? What is\ncourage? The question seems innocent until people discover that a\nconcept can function successfully before anyone can give an adequate\naccount of it. The representation has become visible. The same broad\nmovement appears differently elsewhere. In the Upanishadic traditions,\nritual and inherited cosmology become objects of increasingly radical\ninquiry into self, experience, and ultimate reality. In prophetic\ntraditions, existing authority can be judged by a standard it does not\nitself control. In early natural philosophy, phenomena once embedded in\nmythic agency become candidates for explanation through more general\nprinciples. The important change is not the arrival of thinking. It is a\nchange in the direction of thought. Instead of asking only what\nsomething means within an inherited order, consciousness increasingly\nasks what makes that order intelligible or authoritative in the first\nplace. The gain is enormous. So is the fracture. Once one worldview can\nbe seen as a worldview, alternatives acquire a new force. Different\npeoples possess different gods, laws, rituals, metaphysics, and accounts\nof justice. Their difference can become evidence that one’s own world is\nalso a representation. If they can mistake their map for reality,\nperhaps we can too. At that point plurality is no longer merely social\nor geographical. It has become epistemological. The medieval Christian\nsynthesis can be understood as one immense attempt to solve the problem\nthat follows: can reflection be integrated without returning to naïve\nbelonging? Thomas Aquinas is a particularly clear example. Biblical\nrevelation, Greek philosophy, Aristotelian logic and natural philosophy,\nethics, law, politics, and ordinary social life do not naturally form a\nsingle system. The intellectual ambition is to make them belong to one\nintelligible reality. Reason and revelation cannot be two unrelated\ntruths if both are valid. Apparent contradiction therefore creates\npressure for a deeper reconciliation. The achievement is not simply\nobedience to authority. It is <strong>belonging</strong> <strong>after\nreflection</strong>. The cathedral embodies the same aspiration\nmaterially. Stone, light, hierarchy, ritual, and doctrine become parts\nof a single symbolic environment. The individual does not merely receive\npropositions about the sacred. The person is placed inside an order in\nwhich visible and invisible reality are represented as participating in\none structure. This synthesis is neither pure pre-reflective myth nor\nmodern critical distance. It attempts to contain reflection without\nletting reflection dissolve the world that gives life orientation. But\nno integration is permanent merely because it is comprehensive.\nScientific inquiry, political transformation, religious conflict,\ncommerce, printing, historical criticism, and new forms of individual\nselfhood gradually loosen the synthesis. The result is not simply\nsecularization. Something more consequential occurs: representations\nbecome increasingly specialized, testable, revisable, and detachable\nfrom one another. The mirror multiplies. <strong>Chapter Four — The\nInfinite Mirror</strong> One of modernity’s most powerful achievements\nis the development of representations that can encounter resistance from\nreality in unusually disciplined ways. A scientific model is not\nvaluable because it is austere, mathematical, or modern. It is valuable\nwhen reality can make it fail. Experiments refuse to cooperate.\nPredictions miss. Machines break. Bridges stand or collapse regardless\nof the symbolism attached to them. Operational representation gains\nauthority because the world can say no. This produces extraordinary\nknowledge and agency. Disease can be prevented. Distance can be\novercome. Night can be illuminated. Matter becomes manipulable in ways\nearlier worlds could scarcely imagine. Nature is no longer only an order\nto inhabit; parts of it become systems whose mechanisms can be\nidentified and transformed. A world increasingly experienced as operable\nencourages a self that can operate. Inherited identity says: this is\nwhere you belong. Modern consciousness increasingly asks: what can be\nchanged? Tradition says: this is what you are. The sovereign self asks:\nwhat can I become? This freedom is real, even if never absolute. Class,\nbiology, history, culture, and circumstance continue to constrain human\nlives. But more of the self becomes available to reflection and choice:\noccupation, belief, social role, political allegiance, place, intimate\nlife, identity, and the narrative through which a person understands\ntheir own existence. The old problem returns at a higher intensity. The\nmore representations become visible and revisable, the less obvious it\nbecomes which one should finally orient life. Nietzsche recognized one\nversion of the crisis with extraordinary force. “God is dead” matters\nhere less as a slogan of atheism than as a diagnosis of representational\ninheritance. A civilization can cease to believe in the metaphysical\nstructure that grounded its values while continuing to use the values,\nmoral intuitions, and categories that structure produced. The external\nguarantor disappears before the need for orientation disappears.\nDostoevsky’s Underground Man dramatizes another consequence. Reflection\nbecomes so powerful that every motive can be contaminated by awareness\nof another motive. Action becomes difficult because the self can always\nmove one level outward, reinterpret its desire, expose its vanity, then\nexpose the vanity of the exposure. Consciousness becomes capable of\nseeing through itself faster than it can act. This is the infinite\nmirror. I hold a belief. I ask why. Perhaps it came from my parents. Why\nshould their influence determine me? I choose another belief because it\nfits my values. Where did those values come from? I adopt a theory\nexplaining their origin. Why trust that theory? What social interest or\npsychological need shaped my attraction to it? The mirror can always be\nturned again. Modern media intensifies the process. Experience can be\nrepresented while it is happening. A journey becomes a narrative before\nit ends. A private identity acquires an audience. A person represents\nthemselves; others respond; the response alters self-understanding; the\nnext representation changes; the loop continues. The mirror does not\nmerely reflect the face. The face begins adjusting itself to the mirror.\nCulture becomes reflexive in the same way. Films comment on film\nconventions. Novels comment on the possibility of writing novels.\nAdvertising mocks advertising. Politics anticipates commentary about\npolitical performance. Criticism becomes material for the thing being\ncriticized. Self-awareness can be liberating. But self-awareness does\nnot automatically produce understanding. Recursion can multiply without\ndiscovering anything new. A culture can therefore become extraordinarily\ngood at seeing <em>through</em> representations while becoming less\ncertain what it should see <em>with</em>. The distinction matters\nbecause reflexivity, by itself, gives us no final orientation. The\nresult is not necessarily nihilism. People still love, believe, raise\nchildren, build institutions, practice religions, fight for causes, and\nmake art. But they increasingly do so while alternatives remain visible.\nThe individual inhabits one world while surrounded by evidence that\nother worlds can also be inhabited. What follows is representational\nfragmentation. Science explains mechanism. Psychology explains\nmotivation. Politics organizes collective action. Spirituality offers\ntranscendence. Consumer culture constructs identity. Art offers forms of\nmeaning. Each map may work locally. What becomes uncertain is how the\nmaps belong together. And this returns us to the problem with which the\nbook began. A culture that has spent centuries becoming conscious of\nrepresentation becomes very good at producing representations—including\nrepresentations of depth. The forms associated with profundity become\nrecognizable and reproducible. Recursion acquires an aesthetic.\nAmbiguity acquires prestige. Philosophical language can circulate\nindependently of philosophical discovery. The problem is no longer\nmerely: <strong>How do we see the map?</strong> We are very good at\nseeing maps. The harder question is: <strong>How do we distinguish a\nrepresentation that has actually discovered</strong> <strong>something\nfrom one that has merely learned the appearance of</strong>\n<strong>discovery?</strong> <strong>Part III — Smoke Without\nFire</strong> <strong>Chapter Five — When the Signal Separates from the\nSource</strong> False depth does not usually begin with fraud. The\ncreator may be intelligent. The audience may be moved. The complexity\nmay be real. What is in question is narrower: what operation has\nactually occurred? Several confusions repeatedly interfere with that\nquestion. Complexity can imitate discovery. A large number of\ninteracting elements may demand enormous cognitive labor without\naltering the framework in which those elements operate. Ambiguity can\nimitate inexhaustibility. A work may support several strong\ninterpretations because its structure genuinely sustains tensions that\ncannot be reduced to one reading. But indeterminacy alone proves very\nlittle. A blank wall allows endless projection. That does not make it\nthe deepest artifact in the room. Symbolism can imitate depth. A bird\ncan symbolize freedom, water rebirth, a house the self. The question is\nnot whether a second meaning can be attached to an object. The question\nis what the symbolic relation does inside the larger structure. Does it\nalter other relations? Does it allow previously separated elements to\nbecome intelligible together? Does it remain constrained under\nreinterpretation? Recursion can imitate reflexive discovery. A film can\nreveal the camera. A novelist can write a novelist writing the novel we\nare reading. A character can become aware of their fictionality. Such\ndevices point toward representation, but the presence of a mirror is not\nthe same as discovering something in it. In every case, the central\ndanger is the same: the audience can contribute more intelligence than\nthe artifact constrains. A brilliant critic may produce an extraordinary\ninterpretation of a mediocre object. The interpretation may deserve\nadmiration. But then we must ask whose cognition has been demonstrated.\nThe critic’s intelligence cannot simply be credited backward to the\nartifact. This is why constrained interpretation matters. A strong\nreading is not merely imaginable; it is supported by relationships the\nartifact makes difficult to replace without loss. Reality, formal\nnecessity, or the artifact’s own architecture must be able to resist the\ninterpreter. The same principle applies beyond art. A theory can acquire\nthe appearance of enormous explanatory power by redescribing everything\nin its own vocabulary. If disagreement proves repression, resistance\nconfirms the theory. If absence of evidence proves hidden control, every\noutcome becomes compatible with the framework. Everything connects.\nNothing can escape. But a framework that can explain every possible\nstate of affairs may explain very little. Reality has lost the ability\nto say no. Artificial intelligence brings this old problem into focus\nbecause it weakens another proxy we relied upon: the source behind\nsophisticated representation. For most of history, a difficult argument\nimplied that somebody had constructed the argument. A poem implied that\nsomebody had chosen the words. A philosophical distinction implied that\nsome mind had recognized the distinction. This did not prove truth or\ngenius, but artifact and expected cognitive process were connected\ntightly enough that we rarely questioned the inference. That inference\nis no longer safe. An artificial system can produce an argument without\nyears of concern for the problem. It can write about grief without\ngrieving, imitate the language of faith without belief, and generate\npolished intellectual prose at extraordinary speed. Length is cheap.\nFluency is cheap. Variation is cheap. Even many familiar signs of\nsophistication are becoming cheap. But the opposite conclusion is\nequally invalid. If a machine produces a valid proof, the proof is not\ninvalid because the machine did not experience the discovery as a human\nmathematician would. If it identifies a relation that reality confirms,\nthe relation does not become unreal because its source lacks biography.\nThe origin of a representation and the validity of the operation\ninstantiated in it are different questions. AI therefore does not solve\nthe problem of intelligence. It strips away an old shortcut. We can no\nlonger move automatically from <em>this looks</em>\n<em>intelligent</em> to <em>the expected kind of intelligence must have\noccurred</em> <em>behind it</em>. But neither can we move from <em>a\nmachine produced</em> <em>it</em> to <em>there is no cognition worth\nevaluating here</em>. The evidence therefore has to move toward the\nartifact itself: toward its relationships, constraints, transformations,\nderivations, consequences, and finally toward what it actually does.\nThis shift sounds modest, but it changes the object of evaluation.\n<strong>Part IV — What Happened Here?</strong> <strong>Chapter Six —\nFrozen Consciousness</strong> An artifact remains after the act that\nproduced it disappears. The sentence remains after the thought. The\nproof remains after the mathematician leaves the room. A film survives\nits production. A theory outlives the moment in which its central\nrelation first became visible. What we inherit is not consciousness\nitself. We do not inherit Newton’s subjective experience of insight or\nevery failed path that preceded a result. We do not possess the full\ncognitive history of a novel merely because the finished novel survives.\nCreators forget, rationalize, misdescribe their own process, or discover\npatterns in their work only later. A finished artifact is therefore an\nincomplete record of the mind that produced it. But it is also more\nreliable than speculation about that mind in one crucial respect: it\nshows what survived into structure. A proof externalizes relations.\nAnother mathematician can follow them, test them, reconstruct them,\ndiscover what follows, and sometimes perform part of the original\ncognitive operation again. A scientific theory externalizes an\nexplanatory architecture. A novel stabilizes relationships among\ncharacters, events, language, expectations, symbols, and perspectives. A\nfilm stabilizes relations among image, time, sound, action, omission,\nand consequence. This is what I mean by <strong>frozen\nconsciousness</strong>. The phrase is metaphorical. An artifact is not\nconscious. A theorem does not think. A screenplay does not wait to wake\nwhen somebody opens it. What has been frozen is structure produced by\ncognition. Relationships can cross time. They can become cognitively\nactive in another mind. Representation allows cognition to survive its\nmoment without preserving the experiencing subject that produced it.\nThis explains why creator biography must be handled carefully. Biography\ncan illuminate sources and motives. Intention can clarify local\ndecisions. But neither can replace evidence instantiated in the\nartifact. A creator can give a worse account of the work than the work\ngives of itself because cognition routinely performs operations without\nrepresenting all of them explicitly to itself. A novelist can sense that\nmoving a scene would damage the work without being able to enumerate\nevery relation that would break. The artifact can stabilize what\nreflective self-description could not hold all at once. This suggests a\nnarrower and more defensible question than “How intelligent was the\ncreator?” A person contains capacities that may never enter one work. A\nweak artifact does not prove a weak mind. A great artifact does not\ncontain the total intelligence of the person who made it. The object\nbefore us is narrower, and I think more defensible: <strong>What\ncognition is actually demonstrated here?</strong> Answering it forced a\ndistinction I initially found easy to blur. We need to know both\n<strong>the depth and kind of cognitive operation</strong> the artifact\nsuccessfully performs and <strong>how much cognition</strong> is\ndemonstrated in performing it, including the architecture through which\nthat magnitude is achieved. Those questions are related, but they are\nnot the same. A highly constrained formal artifact can coordinate an\nenormous number of relations, preserve severe constraints, and require\nlong non-routine inference while still operating inside a comparatively\nbounded representational structure. Its cognitive magnitude may\ntherefore be very high without implying that it has achieved a\ncorrespondingly high Operational Order. The two coordinates answer\ndifferent questions. Another artifact may be much shorter and less\nvisibly complicated but reveal that several problems previously treated\nas separate are consequences of one hidden mechanism. The framework\nitself has shifted. Adding more complexity to one achievement does not\nconvert it into the other. This is why the framework needs two\ncoordinates: <strong>Operational</strong>\n<strong>Order</strong> and <strong>Cognitive Magnitude</strong>.\n<strong>Chapter Seven — Operational Order</strong> Operational Order and\nCognitive Magnitude describe two different properties of an artifact.\n<strong>Operational Order asks what cognition achieves.</strong>\n<strong>Cognitive Magnitude asks how much cognition the artifact\ndemonstrates in achieving it.</strong> An artifact can require\nextraordinary cognitive magnitude while remaining at a comparatively low\nOperational Order. Conversely, an artifact can perform a higher-order\noperation without displaying maximal magnitude across every cognitive\ndimension. <strong>Magnitude is not Order.</strong> The Operational\nOrders form the following sequence: <strong>1 → 2 → 3a → 3b → 3c → G →\nGΩ → M</strong> This sequence is hierarchical, but it should not be\nunderstood simply as a scale from unintelligent to intelligent. What\nchanges is the kind of operation cognition performs and, increasingly,\nwhat cognition is capable of making an object of thought. At Level 1,\ncognition operates upon a represented reality. At Level 2, cognition\noperates through organizing representational structures. At Level 3,\ncognition turns recursively upon the relationship between\nrepresentation, consciousness, and reality. At G, cognition relocates\nwhat explains an independently identifiable target. At GΩ, that\nrelocation becomes an unavoidable baseline for adequate successor\ninquiry. At M, cognition establishes a constraint upon the space of\nadmissible explanatory frameworks themselves. The compressed sequence is\nuseful, but it hides the work each transition has to do. The levels\ntherefore need to be examined one by one. <strong>Level 1 — Direct\nConsciousness</strong> <em>The local operation</em> At Level 1,\ncognition operates directly upon objects, events, actions, and relations\nsubstantially as they are represented. A problem appears; relevant\nrelations are identified, possibilities considered, actions selected,\nand consequences followed. Nothing about this makes the cognition\ntrivial. Level 1 does not mean primitive cognition, low intelligence,\nliteral-mindedness, or simplicity. A Level-1 operation can involve\nformidable memory, calculation, anticipation, relational coordination,\ntactical adaptation, or technical skill. What makes it Level 1 is that\nthe dominant achievement remains local to the represented situation. The\nobjects and relations being manipulated do not require a larger symbolic\nor abstract representational system to explain what the artifact\nprincipally accomplishes. A difficult practical solution can therefore\nremain Level 1 even when discovering it requires extraordinary ability.\nThe relevant question is: <strong>Can the artifact’s dominant\nachievement be adequately explained</strong> <strong>through operations\nupon the represented particulars and their</strong>\n<strong>relations?</strong> If so, Level 1 is sufficient. This does not\nmean that Level-1 cognition is somehow free of concepts, language,\nmemory, or learned ways of seeing. Human consciousness is never that\nsimple. The distinction concerns what the artifact accomplishes. If\nunderstanding that achievement does not require us to move from the\nrepresented situation into a larger organizing system of meaning, Level\n1 remains sufficient. However elaborate the operation becomes,\ncomplexity alone does not force the transition upward. <strong>Level 2 —\nSymbolic Consciousness</strong> <em>The mediated operation</em> At Level\n2, cognition no longer operates only upon represented particulars. It\noperates through an organizing representational structure by which those\nparticulars acquire additional significance. That structure may be\nsymbolic, conceptual, mathematical, legal, formal, analogical,\nstrategic, classificatory, or otherwise abstract. Consider legal\nreasoning. A dispute may concern concrete people, actions, property,\npromises, injuries, or events. But sophisticated legal reasoning is not\nexhausted by relations among those particulars. They acquire operative\nsignificance through an abstract system containing such categories as\nprecedent, liability, jurisdiction, intent, ownership, obligation,\nstanding, and evidence. The concrete event is therefore being processed\nthrough a representational structure that extends beyond the event\nitself. The same point becomes clearer when we move across domains. In a\nsymbolic artwork, concrete objects may participate in meanings extending\nbeyond their literal presence. In mathematics, marks and quantities\noperate within formal structures. In music, individual notes can acquire\nfunctions through harmonic, contrapuntal, rhythmic, or transformational\nsystems. In chess, a piece is not merely a physical object occupying a\nsquare. Its significance is constituted by rules, legal possibilities,\npositional relations, strategic structures, threats, exchanges, and\nfuture transformations within the abstract system of the game. Level 2\ncan therefore become cognitively enormous. Its architectures can be\nintricate, highly abstract, deeply constrained, and extraordinarily\ndifficult to construct. But sophistication within a representational\nsystem does not by itself create a higher Operational Order. This is the\n<strong>symbolic ceiling</strong>: a system can become more elaborate\nwithout becoming more recursive in the TORC sense. The distinction\nbetween Levels 1 and 2 can therefore be stated compactly: <em>Level 1\noperates primarily upon represented particulars and their</em>\n<em>relations. Level 2 operates through an organizing\nrepresentational</em> <em>structure by which those particulars acquire\nadditional significance.</em> Level 2 is not established merely because\nsymbols are present. Almost any human artifact can contain symbols. What\nmatters is whether symbolic, abstract, formal, or otherwise mediated\norganization is necessary to explain the artifact’s dominant\nachievement. Nor is Level 3 reached merely because Level 2 becomes\nextraordinarily complex. Complexity, in other words, can expand\nhorizontally without moving vertically. The transition to Level 3 occurs\nonly when the framework through which cognition has been operating\nitself becomes part of the problem. <strong>Level 3 — Recursive\nConsciousness</strong> <em>The reflexive operation</em> Level 3 is where\nthe character of the problem changes. At Level 2, cognition operates\nthrough a framework. At Level 3, the relationship between framework,\nconsciousness, and reality itself becomes an object of operation. The\nstandpoint from which reality is represented can no longer remain\noutside the problem. That is the specific sense in which TORC uses the\nword <em>recursive</em>. It should not be confused with recursion in the\nordinary formal sense. A story within a story, a picture containing\nitself, a self-referential sentence, a recursive mathematical function,\nor a musical structure transforming its own material does not\nautomatically constitute Level 3. TORC recursion is more specific. It\noccurs when cognition begins operating upon the structures through which\ncognition has been representing, interpreting, or orienting itself\ntoward reality. Level 3 has three forms: 3a, 3b, and 3c. They are not\nseparate major orders because they represent progressively deeper\noutcomes of the same recursive transition. <strong>Level 3a — Corrective\nRecursive Consciousness</strong> <em>The correctable fracture</em> At\n3a, consciousness discovers a fracture in the framework through which\nreality has been represented. An institution may systematically distort\nwhat it claims to serve. A moral framework may generate contradictions\ninvisible from within its own assumptions. An ideology may organize\nreality in a way that conceals the conditions it produces. A conceptual\nstructure may prevent something from being represented adequately. The\ncrucial transition is recursive: the structure producing the distortion\nbecomes part of what cognition examines. But the fracture remains, at\nleast in principle, correctable. A false belief can be abandoned. A\ndefective institution can be reorganized. An ideology can be exposed. A\ndistorted representation can be replaced by a more adequate one. The\ncontradiction therefore lies substantially within an alterable\nrelationship between consciousness and its organization of reality. This\ndistinguishes 3a from ordinary criticism. A work does not become 3a\nmerely because it attacks society, condemns injustice, exposes\nhypocrisy, depicts corruption, or proposes reform. The artifact must\nmake the structure producing the distortion part of its dominant\noperation. <strong>representation → discovered distortion → recursive\nexamination →</strong> <strong>possibility of correction</strong> At 3b,\nthat final possibility changes. <strong>Level 3b — Fractured Recursive\nConsciousness</strong> <em>The irreducible fracture</em> At 3b,\nconsciousness encounters a contradiction that cannot simply be repaired\nby representing reality more accurately. The fracture reaches the\nrelationship between consciousness and existence itself. Mortality\ncannot be abolished by understanding mortality correctly.\nIrreversibility cannot be corrected by discovering a better conceptual\nsystem. The separation between self and other cannot simply be\neliminated through more accurate representation. Contingency, finitude,\nincomplete knowledge, loss, conflicting desire, and other existential\nconditions can place consciousness in a situation in which understanding\nthe contradiction does not remove it. Consciousness can see the problem\nand remain inside it. That is the meaning of fractured. The distinction\nfrom 3a is therefore decisive. <strong>At 3a: something about our\norganization of reality can be corrected.</strong> <strong>At 3b: the\ncontradiction survives correction because it belongs to the</strong>\n<strong>relationship between consciousness and existence.</strong> This\ndoes not mean that any artifact about death, suffering, madness,\nalienation, absurdity, or despair qualifies as 3b. <strong>Existential\nsubject matter is not existential operation.</strong> The artifact must\nmake the irreducible fracture structurally necessary to its achievement.\nA tragedy can remain Level 2. A cheerful artifact could, in principle,\nreach 3b. Mood is irrelevant. What matters is whether consciousness has\nconfronted something that improved representation alone cannot repair.\nAnd confrontation is not yet integration. <strong>Level 3c — Integrated\nRecursive Consciousness</strong> <em>A coherent orientation within\ncontradiction</em> At 3c, the contradiction discovered at 3b remains\nreal. The contradiction is neither solved nor exposed as an illusion.\nConsciousness instead reorganizes itself into a coherent orientation\ncapable of containing what it cannot eliminate. Mortality, loss,\nfinitude, limited knowledge, and the distinction between self and world\nremain; what changes is the way consciousness can inhabit them. That is\nwhat I mean here by <strong>integration</strong>. Integration must\ntherefore be distinguished from consolation. A happy ending, hope,\nreligious belief, acceptance, catharsis, or formal closure may accompany\nintegration, but none establishes it by itself. The test is stricter:\n<em>Does the artifact preserve the existential contradiction while</em>\n<em>establishing a coherent orientation capable of containing it?</em>\nIf the contradiction disappears, 3c has not been demonstrated. If the\ncontradiction remains but consciousness merely confronts it, the\nartifact remains 3b. If both contradiction and coherent orientation\nsurvive together, 3c becomes possible. The orientation must also grow\nout of the confrontation itself. It cannot simply be placed on top of\nthe contradiction afterward. A work does not reach integration merely by\nconfronting despair and then adding hope, or by confronting mortality\nand then announcing acceptance. The new orientation has to become\nintelligible through what consciousness has undergone. The contradiction\nchanges how consciousness can inhabit reality, even though the\ncontradiction itself remains. With 3c the recursive family is complete.\nThe next transition is not simply a more intense version of the same\nmovement. <strong>The Generative Threshold</strong> The transition from\n3c to G is not simply another increase in psychological, philosophical,\nsymbolic, or cognitive depth. At Level 3, consciousness transforms its\nrelationship to a problem. At G, what explains the problem changes. No\naccumulation of complexity, abstraction, recursion, symbolism,\noriginality, self-reference, or Cognitive Magnitude can substitute for\nthis requirement. The criterion has changed. <strong>G — Generative\nConsciousness</strong> <em>Explanatory relocation</em> A phenomenon can\nbe observed long before it is adequately explained. It can be described,\nclassified, measured, predicted, represented mathematically, or\nincorporated into sophisticated systems without the structure\nresponsible for its form having been discovered. G occurs when an\nartifact discloses a structure, mechanism, principle, or generative\norganization that changes what explains an independently identifiable\ntarget. The target must exist independently of the artifact’s own formal\nworld. And the newly disclosed structure must do more than redescribe\nit. It must make previously separate, unexplained, or differently\nexplained features intelligible as consequences of a common generative\narchitecture. I call this <strong>explanatory relocation</strong>. The\ndistinction between internal generation and generative consciousness is\nessential. A grammar can generate sentences. An algorithm can generate\nimages. A mathematical rule can generate an infinite sequence. A musical\nsystem can generate compositions. A fictional system can generate an\nelaborate imaginary world. None of these facts alone establishes G. That\nis artifact-internal generativity. G requires something stronger: the\ndisclosed structure explains why an independently identifiable target\ntakes the forms it does. At Level 2, cognition can construct an\nextraordinary representational system. At Level 3, cognition can\nrecursively transform its relationship to reality. At G, cognition\ndiscovers something through which reality—or some independently\nidentifiable part of it—becomes newly explicable. This is why G cannot\nbe inferred from genius, importance, originality, complexity,\nprediction, influence, or a high Cognitive Magnitude Score. The\nexplanatory foundation itself has to move. <strong>GΩ — Generative\nConsciousness: Successor-Baseline Necessity</strong> <em>When\nexplanatory relocation becomes unavoidable</em> Some generative\nachievements go further. After them, adequate inquiry into the target\ncannot simply return to the previous explanatory landscape as though the\ndiscovery had never occurred. TORC marks that stronger case as\n<strong>GΩ</strong>. <strong>G relocates explanation. GΩ makes that\nrelocation an unavoidable</strong> <strong>baseline for adequate\nsuccessor inquiry.</strong> This does not require the original theory to\nremain permanently correct. A successor may revise it, extend it, reject\nparts of it, replace its ontology, introduce new mechanisms, or subsume\nit within a larger framework. But the successor cannot simply restore\nthe old problem-space unchanged. It must answer to what has been\ndiscovered. This gives GΩ a powerful counterfactual test: <em>Could an\nadequate successor reject the original framework and simply</em>\n<em>ignore its explanatory relocation?</em> If yes, GΩ has not been\nestablished. If every adequate successor must reproduce, explain,\ncircumvent, modify, or explicitly answer that relocation, GΩ becomes\npossible. Historical influence is therefore insufficient. Fame is\ninsufficient. Paradigm status is insufficient. Textbook canonization is\ninsufficient. Even G itself is insufficient. GΩ requires structural\nsuccessor necessity. The discovery has changed the starting position\nfrom which adequate future inquiry can proceed. <strong>M — Constitutive\nConsciousness</strong> <em>Constraint upon framework-space</em> M asks\nfor something stronger again. G changes what explains a target. GΩ makes\nthat change unavoidable for adequate successor inquiry. M identifies or\nestablishes a constraint upon the space of admissible frameworks\nthemselves. A generative achievement can be expressed approximately as:\n<strong>This structure explains the phenomenon.</strong> GΩ adds:\n<strong>Adequate successor explanations must reckon with this\nrelocation.</strong> M reaches something stronger: <em>Any admissible\nframework capable of adequately representing or</em> <em>explaining a\ntarget of this kind must satisfy this structural</em>\n<em>condition.</em> Cognition is now operating not merely upon phenomena\nor explanations, but upon the conditions under which explanatory\nframeworks can coherently exist. That is why I use the term\n<strong>constitutive</strong> here. A rival framework may use completely\ndifferent mathematics, concepts, assumptions about reality, or ways of\nrepresenting the problem. But if the M-level constraint is genuine,\nchanging the framework does not make the constraint disappear. The\nconstraint reappears at the level of framework-space itself. M should\ntherefore be assigned exceptionally cautiously. Genius, extreme\nabstraction, historical importance, foundational status, or disciplinary\ntransformation are not enough; even indispensability to later inquiry\nbelongs to GΩ rather than M. What M requires is evidence of structural\nnecessity across admissible frameworks. <strong>What Changes Across the\nOrders?</strong> The hierarchy can now be compressed into a single\nprogression. <strong>Level 1: cognition operates upon represented\nparticulars and their</strong> <strong>relations.</strong> <strong>Level\n2: cognition operates through organizing representational</strong>\n<strong>structures.</strong> <strong>Level 3: cognition recursively\noperates upon the relationship between</strong> <strong>representation,\nconsciousness, and reality.</strong> <strong>G: cognition relocates the\nexplanatory foundation of an independently</strong> <strong>identifiable\ntarget.</strong> <strong>GΩ: that relocation becomes an unavoidable\nbaseline for adequate</strong> <strong>successor inquiry.</strong>\n<strong>M: cognition establishes a structural constraint upon the\nadmissible</strong> <strong>frameworks through which the target can be\nadequately understood.</strong> The progression can be stated another\nway: <strong>things → representations → the relation between\nrepresentation and</strong> <strong>reality → generative explanations →\nsuccessor necessity →</strong> <strong>framework-space\nnecessity</strong> This is why Operational Order and Cognitive Magnitude\nmust remain separate. A Level-2 artifact can display extraordinary\nCognitive Magnitude. Its representational architecture may require\nimmense relational coordination, abstraction, constraint management,\ninferential distance, integrative compression, and generalization. That\ndoes not turn it into Level 3. Likewise, a G artifact is not\nautomatically cognitively larger than every Level-2 or Level-3 artifact.\nG identifies what kind of operation occurred, not the magnitude required\nto perform it. <strong>Operational Order therefore answers: What did the\ncognition</strong> <strong>demonstrated in this artifact\naccomplish?</strong> <strong>Cognitive Magnitude answers: How much\ncognitive magnitude does the</strong> <strong>artifact demonstrate in\naccomplishing it?</strong> The first identifies the operation. The\nsecond measures its magnitude. Together they produce a more complete\ndescription than either could provide alone. They form the two\ncoordinates of the Demonstrated Intelligence Profile. And with\nOperational Order established, the second coordinate can now be\nexamined. <strong>Chapter Eight — Cognitive Magnitude</strong> Two\nartifacts can perform the same kind of operation while differing\nradically in cognitive magnitude. Conversely, a lower-order artifact can\ndisplay greater dimensional complexity than a higher-order one. This is\nwhy Operational Order cannot be converted into a simple intelligence\nscore. Consider a vast novel maintaining dozens of psychologically\ndistinct agents, social systems, historical forces, symbols, temporal\nrelations, and thematic transformations. The work may demonstrate\nstaggering cognitive magnitude while remaining within recursive or\nintegrative consciousness. Now compare it with a compact generative\ntheory that reorganizes a narrower domain through one decisive\nmechanism. The theory may perform the higher-order operation while\ndisplaying less total relational complexity. There is no contradiction\nin that result. Cognitive Magnitude therefore asks a different\nquestion: <strong>how much</strong> <strong>cognition is demonstrated in\nthe successful construction of the</strong> <strong>artifact, and in\nwhat configuration?</strong> This is not merely a description of\narchitectural shape. It is an estimate of demonstrated cognitive\nmagnitude: the amount of consequential cognitive work preserved in the\nartifact’s achieved structure. The artifact cannot reveal the creator’s\nentire mind, but it can provide evidence of the magnitude of cognition\nrequired by what was successfully made. I distinguish six dimensions.\n<strong>Relational Load (RL)</strong> concerns consequential\ninterdependence. A work with high RL is not merely full of elements.\nMany relationships must remain active, and changes propagate. War and\nPeace is demanding not because it contains many names but because\npeople, institutions, histories, motives, families, wars, and social\nexpectations constrain one another across large distances.\n<strong>Abstraction Depth (AD)</strong> concerns how far cognition moves\nfrom concrete instances toward structures, relations among structures,\nand representations of representations. Difficult vocabulary does not\nguarantee abstraction. A simple equation can operate at enormous\nabstraction depth. <strong>Constraint Density (CD)</strong> concerns how\nnarrowly the successful construction space is restricted. How many\nrequirements must be satisfied simultaneously? In a highly constrained\nproof, theory, composition, or narrative, local changes can damage many\nother functions at once. <strong>Inferential Distance\n(ID)</strong> concerns the non-routine transition between what is given\nand what is successfully reached. Many routine steps do not\nautomatically create deep inference. A short transition may be\nextraordinary when the relevant relation is severely underdetermined\nbefore discovery. <strong>Integrative Compression (IC)</strong> concerns\nhow economically a large heterogeneous field is organized while\npreserving the distinctions that matter. This is not brevity. Deleting\ninformation is not compression when the reader must simply invent what\nis missing. Real compression carries more through less. A single\nprinciple can organize phenomena that previously required separate\nexplanations; a single scene can perform narrative, psychological,\nsymbolic, and conceptual functions simultaneously. Compression should\nnot be confused with simplification. If a compact architecture preserves\nfine structure, coordinates a large heterogeneous load, and permits\nsubstantial reconstruction or derivation, the economy is part of the\ncognitive achievement. Multiplicity, length, interpretive plurality, and\nvisible complication do not automatically indicate greater magnitude.\nWhat matters is how much consequential structure the architecture\nactually carries. <strong>Generalization Reach (GR)</strong> concerns\nhow far the demonstrated structure transfers beyond its originating case\nwhile retaining identifiable boundaries. A theory does not gain reach by\nclaiming universality. A narrative insight does not generalize merely\nbecause a critic can metaphorically apply it everywhere. The structure\nhas to travel without being reinvented ad hoc at each destination. These\ndimensions produce an architecture, not merely a total. One artifact may\nbe strongest in abstraction and inferential distance. Another may\ncombine extreme relational load, constraint density, and integrative\ncompression. The aggregate may be similar while the cognitive\nconfigurations are very different. For practical comparison, the six\ndimensions can be summarized in a <strong>Cognitive Magnitude Score\n(CMS)</strong>. CMS is the aggregate measure of the cognitive magnitude\ndemonstrated by the artifact across those six dimensions. The\ndimensional profile preserves the <em>shape</em> of that magnitude; CMS\nsummarizes its overall scale. CMS is calibrated rather than\nmetaphysically exact. A difference of a few points should not be\nmistaken for microscopic certainty, and the six-dimensional architecture\nremains indispensable when totals are close or when two artifacts\nachieve similar magnitude through very different cognitive\nconfigurations. But that caution should not be confused with emptiness:\nCMS is intended to measure something substantive—the demonstrated\ncognitive magnitude required by the artifact’s achieved construction.\nOperational Order and Cognitive Magnitude together form\nthe <strong>Demonstrated Intelligence Profile (DIP)</strong>. The vague\nquestion <em>How intelligent is this artifact?</em> cannot be answered\nadequately by a single number. TORC therefore separates it into two more\nexact questions: <strong>How deep, and of what kind, is the cognitive\noperation the artifact</strong> <strong>successfully performs?</strong>\nAnd: <strong>How much cognition is demonstrated in successfully\nperforming it, and</strong> <strong>what is the architecture of that\ncognition?</strong> Operational Order answers the first. The\nsix-dimensional architecture and CMS answer the second. Together they\nform the artifact’s TORC evaluation. Neither coordinate can do the\nother’s work. A 3c artifact with staggering magnitude does not become G\nthrough accumulation. A G artifact does not automatically exceed every\nLevel-3 artifact on every magnitude dimension. The G artifact has\nachieved the deeper operational disclosure; the 3c artifact may\nnevertheless demonstrate greater cognitive magnitude. Complexity cannot\nmanufacture explanatory relocation, and explanatory relocation does not\nerase the magnitude of complexity. Keeping that separation intact is one\nof the framework’s central commitments. <strong>Chapter Nine — The\nGenerator and the Test</strong> A framework designed to distinguish\ngenuine cognitive operation from its appearance faces an obvious danger:\nit can become very good at confirming itself. Choose only favorable\nexamples and almost any theory appears powerful. Describe every artifact\nin the theory’s own vocabulary and the vocabulary begins to look\nuniversal. If TORC is useful, it must survive cases in which reputation,\ncomplexity, historical influence, truth, and operational achievement\npull in different directions. The most important test concerns G because\napparent generativity is easy to manufacture. This was one of the places\nwhere the framework most needed resistance from its own examples: if\nevery impressive reframing counted as generative, G would collapse into\na compliment. A candidate generator must do more than arrange phenomena\ninside a striking new picture. It must generate nontrivial explanatory\nconsequences concerning an independently identifiable target. The target\nmust be stateable without simply naming the artifact’s own invented\nworld. And the relevant phenomena must constrain the framework rather\nthan being defined into agreement with it. This is where <strong>false\ndisclosure</strong> appears. The most dangerous false explanation is not\none that explains nothing. It is one that becomes increasingly capable\nof explaining everything. Contradictions become further evidence.\nMissing evidence becomes proof of hidden operation. Every possible\noutcome can be absorbed. The system acquires enormous apparent coherence\nbecause nothing is allowed to count against it. Such a framework may\ndemonstrate abstraction, integration, rhetorical power, and even\nimpressive internal compression. But it has become explanatorily closed.\nReality no longer resists. A genuine generator is powerful partly\nbecause it takes risks. It excludes possibilities. It creates\nexpectations. It explains some patterns better than others. It can fail.\nThat principle applies differently across domains. In science,\nresistance may be empirical. A prediction misses; an experiment\nconflicts with the model; a mechanism fails to account for an observed\ncase. In mathematics, resistance may be formal. A proof does not survive\ninvalid inference. A proposed construction violates constraints that\ncannot be negotiated rhetorically. In philosophy, resistance may involve\ncontradiction, counterexample, conceptual insufficiency, or failure to\naccount for cases the framework claims to organize. In narrative art,\nresistance is more complicated but still real. A claimed explanatory\nstructure must be carried by the work rather than supplied mainly by the\ncritic. It should explain otherwise distinct features of character,\naction, form, and consequence without turning every ambiguity into\nautomatic confirmation. If removing the proposed structure leaves the\nwork just as intelligible, the structure is probably not the dominant\noperation. This is also where prestige must disappear from the\nevaluation. Newton does not qualify because Newton is Newton. If the\nname vanished and the mathematical relocation remained, the argument\nshould survive. Darwin does not qualify because later generations called\nhim a genius. The mechanism must do the work. The same discipline must\napply to art. A canonical novel does not receive a higher classification\nbecause scholars have written thousands of pages about it. A new\nscreenplay does not receive a lower classification because nobody has\nheard of it. An AI-generated proof does not fail because its source\nlacks human biography. A sacred text does not qualify because millions\norganize their lives around it. The classification has to be earned by\nthe evidence in the artifact. That produces difficult cases. I take this\nas a feature rather than a defect: a framework that never has to\nhesitate is probably protecting itself from the objects it claims to\nevaluate. A scientifically false theory can still demonstrate a genuine\ngenerative operation if it successfully relocates explanation under real\nconstraints, even if later inquiry discovers that the mechanism is\nincomplete or wrong. Operational achievement and ultimate truth are not\nidentical. A beautiful and psychologically inexhaustible novel can\nremain at Level 3 while displaying extraordinary Cognitive Magnitude.\nThat is not an insult. The classification says what operation was\nachieved, not whether the work deserves love. A technically ugly paper\ncan contain a major generator. Style does not rescue or disqualify the\noperation. A work can be enormously influential without becoming GΩ.\nInstitutions can make an idea historically unavoidable without making it\nexplanatorily necessary. An interpreter can discover a magnificent\nstructure not sufficiently constrained by the artifact. In that case the\ninterpretation may demonstrate more cognition than the object\ninterpreted. These are not peripheral annoyances. They are where the\nframework has to prove that its distinctions do real work. The original\nquestion was: why do some works genuinely reorganize understanding while\nothers produce the appearance of having done so? The answer, if TORC is\nuseful, cannot be another synonym for genius. It has to change what we\nlook for. We look for the operation instantiated in the artifact. We ask\nwhat relationships it establishes, what constraints it survives, what\ntransformations it performs, what follows from them, and whether reality\nor formal structure can push back. We distinguish the cognitive\nmagnitude of construction from the order of the operation that\nconstruction achieves. The surface is no longer enough. Time, prestige,\ndifficulty, canon, fluency, and complexity may all provide clues,\nsometimes important ones, but none establishes the underlying operation.\nNone, by itself, is the fire. <strong>Epilogue — What Still\nBurns</strong> Human beings will continue to live through\nrepresentations. There is no world available to consciousness after maps\ndisappear, because maps are part of what makes a world intelligible in\nthe first place. Nor is recursion something we can simply undo. Once a\nrepresentation has become visible as representation, naïve return is\nimpossible. We can inhabit traditions after questioning them, but not in\nexactly the same way as before. We can commit while knowing that\nalternatives exist. We can use models while knowing they are models. The\ntask is not to escape representation or recover some lost world before\nthe mirror. It is to become better at distinguishing what our\nrepresentations actually accomplish. The problem extends well beyond\ncriticism—to science and philosophy, certainly, but also to politics,\nreligion, education, and artificial intelligence. It belongs to any\nculture in which signals of understanding can circulate more quickly\nthan understanding itself. Representation is one of consciousness’s\ngreatest powers because it allows cognition to travel. A relation\ndiscovered by one mind can become available to another centuries later.\nA proof can be reconstructed. A story can reorganize perception. A\ntheory can change the questions a field is able to ask. Human\nconsciousness dies; some of its operations survive. But representation\npreserves error as faithfully as insight. Smoke can outlive fire. A\nvocabulary can survive after the discovery that gave it force has\ndisappeared. A style can continue after its necessity is gone. A culture\ncan inherit the appearance of transformation. That is why the question\nremains useful: <strong>What happened here?</strong> Prestige,\ndifficulty, interpretive abundance, and the presumed intelligence of the\ncreator can all distract from the harder question: <strong>What\noperation has actually been demonstrated, and how much\ncognition</strong> <strong>is instantiated in carrying it out?</strong>\nThe answer will never be perfectly mechanical. No serious account of\ncognition should pretend that judgment can be eliminated. But judgment\ncan be disciplined. Signals can be separated from operations. Magnitude\ncan be separated from order. Interpretation can be asked to show its\nconstraints. Explanations can be required to encounter resistance. The\npoint is not to reduce art, thought, or human intelligence to a score.\nIt is to stop mistaking the smoke for the fire. And to become more\nprecise about what still burns.</p>\n";

function enhanceBookHtml(input:string){
 return input
  .replace(/<p><strong>Personal Motivation<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-front-heading"><strong>Personal Motivation</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>(Part [IVX]+ — [^<]+)<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-part"><strong>$1</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>(Chapter [^<]+)<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-chapter"><strong>$1</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>(Epilogue — [^<]+)<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-chapter book-epilogue"><strong>$1</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>What has actually been accomplished\?<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-emphasis"><strong>What has actually been accomplished?</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p>Smoke can exist without fire\.\s*The mask can learn the face\.<\/p>/g,'<p class="book-emphasis">Smoke can exist without fire. The mask can learn the face.</p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>Successful representation tends to disappear into what it\s*makes visible\.<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-emphasis"><strong>Successful representation tends to disappear into what it makes visible.</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>Complexity can expand horizontally without moving vertically\.<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-emphasis"><strong>Complexity can expand horizontally without moving vertically.</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p><strong>The explanatory foundation itself must move\.<\/strong><\/p>/g,'<p class="book-emphasis"><strong>The explanatory foundation itself must move.</strong></p>')
  .replace(/<p>None is the fire\.<\/p>/g,'<p class="book-emphasis">None is the fire.</p>');
}
const styledBody=enhanceBookHtml(body);

export default function Book(){
 return <main className={styles.bookPage}>
  <header className={`page-title ${styles.bookTitlePage}`}>
   <h1>The Age of False Depth</h1>
   <p className="lede">Intelligence, Representation, and What Artifacts Actually Do</p>
   <p className={styles.theoryName}>A Theory Of Representational Consciousness</p>
   <p className={styles.authorLine}>by <b>G. K. Muijlaert</b></p>
  </header>
  <section className={`section prose auditReport ${styles.bookReader}`}>
   <div className={`auditNote ${styles.editionNote}`}><b>Living edition.</b> This online text is the current canonical version. It may be refined as TORC is tested against additional artifacts and boundary cases.</div>
   <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{__html:styledBody}} />
  </section>
 </main>
}